  "name": "Question 2",
  "problem": "Simplify $\\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(\\frac{\\pi }{10})\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(\\frac{\\pi }{10})}{\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(\\frac{\\pi }{5})}.$",
  "worked_solution": "First, let's recall the double-angle formula for sine: \n\n$$\n2\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x=\\operatorname{sin}\u20612x\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=\\frac{\\pi }{10}$ into the above, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(\\frac{\\pi }{10})\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(\\frac{\\pi }{10}) & =\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{10}) \\\\ 2\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(\\frac{\\pi }{10})\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(\\frac{\\pi }{10}) & =\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(\\frac{\\pi }{5})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can rewrite the given expression using the above, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(\\frac{\\pi }{10})\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(\\frac{\\pi }{10})}{\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(\\frac{\\pi }{5})} & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(\\frac{\\pi }{5})}{\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(\\frac{\\pi }{5})}=\\operatorname{tan}\u2061(\\frac{\\pi }{5})\\end{aligned}\n$$",
